# Sanitization: cleaning user input (XSS prevention)

Package: `Pervaxis.Genesis.Sanitization` (already referenced and registered in this service).

Sanitize free-text values that come from a user and are later stored or shown as text, such as comments, notes, names
and descriptions. Choose the fields on purpose: sanitization changes the value.

## What the platform already does

| Protection | Where | Default |
|---|---|---|
| Settings read from `Genesis:Sanitization` in `appsettings.json` | `Program.cs` | On |
| `[Sanitize]` properties are cleaned before validation | Global filter from `AddGenesisSanitization` | On |
| JSON bodies larger than `MaxInputLength` are rejected with **413** | `Infrastructure/Middleware/JsonBodyLimitMiddleware.cs` | On |
| Every string in every JSON POST, PUT and PATCH body is cleaned, including nested values | `app.UseGenesisSanitization()` | **Off** (`EnableMiddleware: false`) |

Why the body limit: the sanitizer refuses strings longer than `MaxInputLength`. Without the limit, the body-wide
middleware would pass such a request through **unsanitized**, and a `[Sanitize]` property would fail with 500.
A JSON string cannot be longer than its body, so capping the body closes both. File uploads (multipart) are not affected.

## Profiles

| Profile | Result |
|---|---|
| `PlainText` | All HTML removed. This is the default. |
| `SafeHtml` | Safe tags such as bold, italic, links and lists are kept. Scripts, event handlers and iframes are removed. |
| `Markdown` | Everything in `SafeHtml`, plus code blocks, headings, blockquotes, tables and images. |

Custom profiles can be added in configuration (`CustomProfiles`).

### What `PlainText` does to a value

- Removes script and style content, then anything that looks like a tag (`<...>`). `5 < 6 and 7 > 3` becomes `5 3`.
- Decodes HTML entities: `&amp;` becomes `&`.
- Collapses all whitespace, including line breaks, to single spaces and trims the ends.

So **never** apply it to passwords, secrets, tokens, codes, identifiers, raw JSON or XML, or multi-line text whose line
breaks matter. Leave those fields unmarked, or use `SafeHtml` / `Markdown` where formatting is wanted.

## Settings

Read from the `Genesis:Sanitization` section of `appsettings.json`. The generated values are shown.

```json
{
  "Genesis": {
    "Sanitization": {
      "DefaultProfile": "PlainText",
      "MaxInputLength": 1000000,
      "EnableMiddleware": false,
      "MiddlewareExcludedRoutes": []
    }
  }
}
```

`MaxInputLength` is both the longest string the sanitizer accepts and the largest JSON body the service accepts.

## Option 1: mark the property on the request model (simplest)

The package runs a filter before validation and cleans every property marked with `[Sanitize]`.

```csharp
using Pervaxis.Genesis.Sanitization.Filters;

namespace {{ model.names.namespace }}.Domains.Comments.Models;

public sealed record CreateCommentRequest
{
    // SafeHtml keeps simple formatting.
    [Sanitize(Profile = "SafeHtml")]
    public required string Body { get; init; }

    // No profile means the default profile (PlainText).
    [Sanitize]
    public required string AuthorName { get; init; }
}
```

Limits of Option 1:
- It only cleans **top-level string properties** of the action's arguments. A string inside a nested object or a list is not touched, even if it has `[Sanitize]`. Option 3 (`Sanitized`) also only works on top-level properties. Use Option 2, or the middleware, for nested values.
- It only runs for values that are not `null`.

## Option 2: call the sanitizer in the service

Use this when the value does not come straight from a request model, or is nested.

```csharp
using Pervaxis.Genesis.Sanitization.Abstractions;

namespace {{ model.names.namespace }}.Domains.Comments.Services;

public sealed class CommentService(ISanitizer sanitizer) : ICommentService
{
    public string CleanBody(string body) => sanitizer.SanitizeHtml(body)!;        // keeps safe HTML

    public string CleanTitle(string title) => sanitizer.StripAll(title)!;         // plain text only

    public string CleanCustom(string body) => sanitizer.Sanitize(body, "MyCustomProfile")!;  // named profile
}
```

`ISanitizer` methods: `StripAll`, `SanitizeHtml`, `Sanitize(input, profileName)` and `Sanitize(input, profile)`. All of them return `null` for `null` input.
{{- if model.manifest.tenant_ready }}
Store the cleaned values through the service's repository or `{{ model.names.dot_net_class_name }}DbContext` as usual (see `TenancyQuickStart.md`). Do not create a feature `DbContext`.
{{- end }}

## Option 3: inside a FluentValidation validator

Two modes: clean the value (`Sanitized`) or reject dirty input (`MustBeSanitized`).

```csharp
using FluentValidation;
using Pervaxis.Genesis.Sanitization.Abstractions;
using Pervaxis.Genesis.Sanitization.Extensions;

namespace {{ model.names.namespace }}.Domains.Comments.Validators;

public sealed class CreateCommentRequestValidator : AbstractValidator<CreateCommentRequest>
{
    public CreateCommentRequestValidator(ISanitizer sanitizer)
    {
        // Clean the value, then validate the cleaned value.
        RuleFor(x => x.Body)
            .Sanitized(sanitizer, "SafeHtml")
            .NotEmpty();

        // Reject the request if the value contains anything that would be removed.
        RuleFor(x => x.AuthorName)
            .MustBeSanitized(sanitizer);
    }
}
```

## Option 4: clean every JSON body (the middleware)

`app.UseGenesisSanitization()` is already in `Program.cs`, but does nothing until `EnableMiddleware` is `true`.
When on, it cleans **every** string in **every** JSON POST, PUT and PATCH body with `DefaultProfile`, including values in
nested objects and lists. That includes fields such as passwords, so before turning it on:

- List the routes whose bodies must arrive unchanged in `MiddlewareExcludedRoutes` (exact paths, or a prefix ending in `*`).
- Check every field of the remaining endpoints against "What `PlainText` does" above.
- Ask the platform team; it changes every endpoint.

## Which option to use

- Specific free-text fields of request models: Option 1.
- Values built or loaded inside a service, or nested values: Option 2.
- You already have a validator and want the rule next to the others: Option 3.
- A service whose every body is display text and that has no secrets in its bodies: Option 4.
- Do not apply the same property to more than one option. It is wasted work.

## Sanitizing input does not replace encoding output

Always encode a value for the place it is shown. Angular templates escape bound values by default; never put stored
values into `innerHTML` or `bypassSecurityTrust*`. Encode values in e-mails, PDFs, CSV exports and log viewers as well.

## Tests

`GenesisModuleTests.cs` checks the settings binding, `[Sanitize]`, the middleware on and off (including nested values),
the 413 body limit and the validator rules. Keep them.
{{- end -}}
